I would like to request an option to allow users to disable exceptions while using the CLI11 library.

Many projects are using `-fno-exceptions` to reduce the performance overhead and binary sizes, especially after `std::expected` is introduced in C++23. 

The biggest problem for using this library with `-fno-exceptions` comes from the GCC compilation. It seems that GCC simply forbids the usage of `throw` at all when using `-fno-exceptions`. Clang, on the other hand, is fine as long as runtime path doesn't hit `throw`.

For example, I'm getting this error with gcc:

```text
~/.conan2/p/cli115c29056d7e51f/p/include/CLI/impl/Encoding_inl.hpp: In function ‘void CLI::detail::set_unicode_locale()’:
~/.conan2/p/cli115c29056d7e51f/p/include/CLI/impl/Encoding_inl.hpp:43:76: error: exception handling disabled, use ‘-fexceptions’ to enable
   43 |     throw std::runtime_error("CLI::narrow: could not set locale to C.UTF-8");
``` 

Another issue would be about dealing with the help option, which is based on the exception handling. For now, there seems no easy way other than remove the build-in help option and create my own version of the help option using callbacks and flags.

