Currently, as far as I understand it, container-like options _always_ consume as many arguments as possible ((up to a bound set by `expected()`)). However, this greedy behavior still occurs when `delimiter()` is set, which becomes very unintuitive in combination with ((optional)) positional arguments. 

Consider a very simple example:
```cpp
std::filesystem::path inputPath{};
app.add_option(
	 "input_path",
	 inputPath
)->check(CLI::ExistingDirectory);

std::vector<std::string> strings{};
app.add_option("-s", strings)->delimiter(',');
```
If we call `program /some/dir -s abc,def` we get the intended behavior of `inputPath = "/some/dir"` and `strings = {"abc", "def"}` ((though the default help message techincally forbids passing positional arguments before options, so the user would need to figure that out first)), while calling the much more intuitive `program -s abc,def /some/dir` results in the _unintended_ `inputPath = ""` and `strings = {"abc", "def", "/some/dir"}`. This latter case the the one I'm concerned with, because even though the delimiter is conceptually binding the two arguments much more strongly, the following argument is still matched greedily and included into the container instead of the positional argument.

My proposal is for either the default behavior of `delimiter()` to disable this greedy matching, or to add a flag that allows disabling it ((as well as updating the default help text to display the delimiter)). From my perspective, there is no situation in which an end user calling `program -s abc,def /some/dir` _ever_ intends for `/some/dir` to be part of the arguments of `-s`, and I think just fully disallowing that case ((potentially even with an error)) would make the program behavior much less surprising to an end user. 

Potentially, setting a delimiter should just fully disable the ability to pass multiple arguments _without_ using the delimiter ((this could probably be implemented by simply only matching the argument directly after the option flag if `delimiter()` is set)), though that might be too much of a breaking change

When using CLI::CheckedTransformer and parsing an enum that has an `operator<<` overload for `ostream`, CLI11 is throwing a `CLI::ConversionError` when a `.default_val` is defined. I am using release v2.6.2.

The full code to trigger this is below, but here is an explanation:

```cpp
app.add_option("--color", color)
    ->default_val(Color::Red)
    ->transform(CLI::CheckedTransformer(STR_TO_COLOR_MAP));
```

The `STR_TO_COLOR_MAP` maps valid input strings to the enum. When no `std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, Color c)` is defined, this works correctly. When defining the overload (which converts the enum to string representing it), however, a ConversionError is thrown when attempting to parse the default_val.

```c++

#include "CLI11.hpp"
#include <unordered_map>

#define COLOR_LIST \
    X(Red) \
    X(Blue) \
    X(Green)  \

enum class Color {
#define X(name) name,
  COLOR_LIST
#undef X
};

const std::unordered_map<std::string, Color> STR_TO_COLOR_MAP {
#define X(name) {#name, Color::name},
  COLOR_LIST
#undef X
};

const std::unordered_map<Color, std::string> COLOR_TO_STR_MAP {
#define X(name) {Color::name, #name},
  COLOR_LIST
#undef X
};


int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
    CLI::App app{"Example"};

    Color color;

    app.add_option("--color", color)
        ->default_val(Color::Red)
        ->transform(CLI::CheckedTransformer(STR_TO_COLOR_MAP));

    CLI11_PARSE(app, argc, argv);

    std::cout << static_cast<int>(color) << std::endl;
}

// Comment this to stop CLI::ConversionError from being thrown
std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, Color c) {
    auto it = COLOR_TO_STR_MAP.find(c);
    if (it != COLOR_TO_STR_MAP.end())
        return os << it->second;

    return os << std::string("Unknown");
}

```

```sh
g++ -std=c++17 main.cpp -o main
```

Note that I also tried using `->default_str("Green")`, (instead of default_val), and although there is no error thrown, the value written to the variable appears to be random (not the expected underlying integer value of 0,1, or 2)

### MWE:
```cpp
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
	CLI::App app{"App description"};

	int someNumber;
	app.add_option("someNumber,-n,--someNumber", "Some number")
		->check(CLI::PositiveNumber)
		->default_val(0);
	CLI11_PARSE(app, argc, argv);
	return 0;
}
